From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
Date: Tue, 20 Sep 94 23:19:24 WET DST
Subject: [#94-9-334] SCI: role for Q.M in neurobiology

Doug Cutrell writes:
> 2) Recent research on "quantum computation" suggests that quantum mechanics
> may simply be *qualitatively* better at solving the same sorts of problems
> that can *also* be solved with classical computation... for example, Shor's
> results on factoring in polynomial time with quantum computers.  This has
> nothing to do with the issue of non-computability.

   This has been known for a while and is even true of classical
"physical computers" See A.K. Dewdney's the "Armchair Universe" and
the chapter on analog gadgets. For instance, one can "compute"
the complex hull of a set of points (no matter how large) in
constant time. The way this is visualized is as follows. Draw
the points on a piece of wood. Place nails in each point. Now,
put a rubber band around the outside and it will quickly form
the complex hull. The "universe" can also solve differential
equations, cubic equations, finding the maximum path in a graph,
etc in similar times.  (not to mention an O(1) sort of a set
no matter how large)

   The criticism of these neat tricks is, are they practical?
Shor's quantum factoring paper is surely a tour de force in
mathematical manipulation, the question is, can you build one of
these suckers? The answer, given by many, is "probably not."
While his result is theoretically interesting, in practice,
a quantum computer would lose many of its advantages.

  The implication for "quantum biology" is analogous: sure, in theory,
quantum mechanical computers could provide a great performance
boost for bit storage and speed, the question is, given
the environment of cell machinery, an environment with lots of
thermal noise, radiation, etc, will the quantum effects be
statistically relevant? My own personal opinion, having lots of
ignorance on the subject, is that the mechanical and chemical
processes which go on in the brain and life swamp any potential
signal the esoteric quantum effects could contribute. If they are
there, they form the basis of rules of chemistry only, and thus
they are no more relevant to consciousness than to the combustion
in your car's engine. That is, they provide no additional benefits
to living systems. Nature has shown itself to be a bad innovator
when it comes to harnassing extra physical knowledge. I contend
that evolution will choose the easiest path first. If non-conscious
not quantum based neurology were A, and quantum based neurology were
B, I say A is relatively easy to get to, but B is literally
so far away that it is improbable that ordinary evolution will
get a chance to do it before the universe goes away. For similar
reasons, nature evolved gliders and wing based animals before
it evolved a creature with a built in RL-10 rocket engine.
(or, nature uses primitive electrochemical effects much earlier
than it evolves an intergrated circuit based biology) Thus,
we don't see many quantum transistors in nature, and I argue
that they are "hard" for nature to develop.


[rest deleted]

  Personally, I see this as nothing more than an attempt to bring
back vitalism. To prove via quantum hackery that "life" is more
special and requires "the real thing" and hence can't be simulated.
I find Penrose's arguments uncompelling and I see it as nothing more
than an attempt to add mysticism to consciousness. Let's assume
that quantum computation plays a part in solving difficult
NP problems needed for life. There's nothing stopping us from
building our own quantum computers. Upon doing this, I bet
Penrose would come up for yet another excuse for why machines
can't be conscious (such as his bogus Godelian arguments)
 
  It's not surprising that you find a lot of crackpots latching onto
QM computers such as Jack Sarfatti. (or even Brian Josephson)
We have now reached a point where people are trying to use
the EPR paradox to explain telepathy in humans, or vacuum
polarization to explain the "soul"

  BTW, I'm not saying that QM computers couldn't be of use in biology.
Most plausible to me is the role of "analog computation"
(either QM or classical) being used to process sensory information
such as in the inner ear or in the layers of the retina. However,
I don't think this generalizes, just as I don't think the brain
is completely connectionist or completely symbol manipulative.
I think the brain is a hodge podge of several techniques 
compiled over tens of millions of years of evolution. Connectionism
in the older brain for our good processing of environmental information,
but symbol heuristics for language and other high order thought
processes (goals, beliefs, rational thought, etc. even if this
is built on top of a connectionist substrate).

   Anyway, even if quantum computation is used by the brain for
"consciousness", this doesn't help Penrose's thesis. A classical
computer can still do what a quantum computer can do, albeit, more
slowly. Thus, if we accept the hypothesis that our brains
use QM computation, if follows that we may build them classically
too. (quantum computation hasn't solved any NP complete
problems yet)

-Ray


