>for it.)

[etc.]
>
>So where does he go wrong?  Mainly he keeps piling on unlikely
>possibilities until his total scenario is quite unlikely, though still
>possible in principle.  The only reason for entertaining such a
>scenario he offers is his Godelian proof that math folk "understand"
>math in a way computers couldn't possibly.  I didn't get to reading
>his new proof, but I doubt if he can make it fly.  I didn't find
>anyone familiar with Godelian math who though his last proof flew.

There is absolutely no, repeat, no merit in his old worn-out repetition of
Lucas wrong argument about mathematicians being able to recognize truth
when machines cannot.  BECAUSE:

Hidden just under the surface is THE ASSUMPTION THAT WHEN A MATHEMATICIAN
"INTUITS" THE TRUTH OF A THEOREM, HE/SHE IS NEVER WRONG. Well, a good
looking argument with a false assumption is precisely as good as no
argument at all.

All that Penrose and Lucas, and all of them have said is that they can
sometimes GUESS the truth.  Well, big deal.

