Two questions concerning the general topic covered by Penrose, who I
must admit to not having read entirely...

1.  Concerning intuition, doesn't this call up the distinction between
the context of discovery and the context of justification?  Concerning
Penrose (and general arguments of this nature), I thought the point was
not that computers were unable to prove a theorem, but that they were
unable to make that leap of intuition (since intuition is assumed to be
something inherently unformalizable and hence incapable of being
'taught' to a computer) that tells us what the theorem might be, the
next step being to try to prove the product of that leap in some sort of
logical manner.

2.  Has there yet been a refutation of Searle's Chinese Room theory?

Concerning both questions, my asking them may be a product of not having
looked at the literature in a few years, so if this is in any
repetitious or anything, please forgive.

Darren

