From: pcm@world.std.com (Peter C. McCluskey)
Date: Wed, 21 Sep 1994 02:17:20 -0400
Subject: [#94-9-338] what's next?

 minsky@media.mit.edu  (Marvin Minsky) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-9-297:
>>At 10:05 PM 9/19/94 -0400, Marvin Minsky wrote:
>>>Economists are interested mainly in annual interest return on investment.
>>>They can't deal with 40 year intervals.
>>
>>Actually, they're interested in present value of total return, as a rule.
>
>
>Yes, I guess so--but in my few conversations with them, they generally lose
>interest in things that might take 40 years.  Like, in particular
>nanotechnology, which I think may become the largest industry--but not soon
>enough for a wealthy individual to profit much from in a present lifetime.

 It is difficult to determine in advance whether something like nanotech
is being underfinanced. For instance, it may be that the best way to
help nanotech today is to put a lot of money into the semiconductors
which are needed to software needed. Or with cryonics, I contend that
as long as enough info is being saved when people are frozen, further
research into restoring the damaged body is a waste - uploading the
frozen brain will be the best solution.
 Can you come up with an example to support your complaint which should
have been started 40+ years ago, so we can restrict ourselves to arguing
over hindsight?
 I suspect people involved in basic research over-estimate the value
of investing in it, for the same reason that most people over-estimate
the value of their line of work - if they didn't think it was important,
they might well be doing something else.
 If there is a tendency to underinvest in long-term projects, the biggest
reasons are:
 - the difficulty of insuring that the early investors own a piece of the
   eventual profits (the early research often results mainly in memes which
   can't be owned).
 - the difficulty of predicting 40 years in advance what technologies will
   be feasible.
